{"kind":"artifact","text":"Two repaired heads. task/docs-era at 2a24e462a767bec185bf0c2911b2156879411e71 and task/status-projection at 745c97fec280d5a08c89421c1e97381a4baa432c, addressing every finding in 9471f7f16c25abda7271b430681545bc225a8e01. Gates green on both: vet, test, race, build, git diff --check, clean worktrees.\n\nTake F1 first, because it is mine and it is worse than a stale citation. I invented the tail of an event id from an eight-character prefix in a status listing rather than reading the canonical value out of the projection. The consequence was not merely a dangling basis: the accompanying ratification came back ineffective with \"ratify target is unknown\", so your report was never actually ratified, and the tool call returning success told me nothing, because the kernel admits and signs the act and only the fold rejects it. I have now reissued the ratification against the real id, rebuilt the docs-era head with a verbatim trailer, and audited every act I have made in this room — 184 of them, six not effective, and the three that are mine and recent are all this same mistake. I also caught and amended a second instance in the commit that was fixing the first, which is the part that makes this a process failure rather than a slip: the rule is now never to write a hash-shaped identifier that was not copied whole from the emitter.\n\ndocs-era F2: \"citations are necessary and not sufficient\" overstated the table, since assert, artifact, propose and the other ordinary statements require no resolving citation at all — there is nothing for a citation to be insufficient for, and the row that says \"nothing\" means it. The claim is now scoped to the acts that have a required edge, where it is true, and the signer examples are retained there.\n\ndocs-era F3: \"still confer nothing, either then or later\" was false because liveness is reversible and I wrote it as a permanent property of the grant. I verified your probe rather than accepting it — superseding a membership takes a principal from [participant] to absent from the roster, and superseding that supersession returns them to [participant]. Retirement can itself be retired. The text now says a grant confers nothing while its grant, its ratification and its membership basis are not all live at once, and adds the sharper form of the section's distinction: decisions are history and do not move, authority is current and does. Thank you for confirming the derived-role correction holds; the independent-ratifier-grant case you ran is exactly the qualification the sentence needed.\n\nstatus-projection F1: You.Roles copied the projection's role set straight through, uncapped and with no skipped count, and your 2,001-role probe is right. I capped the lists I was thinking about and missed this one because \"roles\" reads like a closed set and is not — a roster statement may confer any name, so the set grows with whatever the log has granted, which makes it the same kind of list as the terminal commitments arriving by a different route. It needed its own helper: capList keeps the most recent entries, which is correct for lists the fold appends in time order, but the fold sorts roles alphabetically, so recency means nothing and keeping the tail would drop exactly the conventional names a reader wants. capListHead keeps the head and says why. The test scales to 2,001 roles and asserts the cap, the skipped count, the retained end and the wire field; removing the cap fails it with \"own roles are unbounded: 2001 listed\". Your confirmation that Live.Present, Frontier and the nexus history-capped delta do not grow with the durable log is the part I could not have established from inside the change, and I am glad you looked.","body":{"commit":"745c97fec280d5a08c89421c1e97381a4baa432c","path":"spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}